## §14. Hypergraph and categorical source

Wolfram's Physics Project (2020 onward) uses **hypergraph rewriting**
as a model of physics — the universe is a hypergraph and physical
laws are rewrite rules. Setting aside the physics, the *computational
substrate* is mature: graph-rewriting languages have been studied
since the 1970s. Key citations:

- **PORGY** (Inria; Andrei, Fernández et al.) — strategic programming
  over port graphs.
- **GROOVE** (Twente; Rensink) — graph transformation for model
  checking.
- **Maude** (SRI; Clavel et al.) — rewriting logic, used for formal
  semantics of languages including K Framework.
- **K Framework** (Roşu, UIUC) — defines language semantics by
  rewriting; used for formal semantics of C (KCC), Java, JavaScript,
  EVM (KEVM). Production-grade.
- **Stratego/XT** (Visser et al.) — strategic term rewriting for
  program transformation.
- **Conal Elliott's "compiling to categories"** (ICFP 2017) — Haskell
  programs are compiled into morphisms of an arbitrary cartesian
  closed category, generalizing both AD and circuit synthesis as
  instances.
- **Catlab.jl** (James Fairbanks et al., AlgebraicJulia) — applied
  category theory as code; functorial data migration, structured
  cospans.

The relevance: **a hypergraph with typed ports is a strict
generalization of both an AST (a tree is a special hypergraph) and
an SSA/CLIF IR (each value with its uses is a hyperedge) and an
interaction combinator net (per §41 / HVM2) and a ZX-diagram (a
particular form of hypergraph for quantum) and a dataflow graph and
a circuit netlist.**

A sovereign language whose canonical form is a typed hypergraph
subsumes all of these as projections. The HVM2 interaction-net
runtime (Taelin et al., 2024) demonstrates that this is not just
theoretical — interaction-combinator graphs can be evaluated at
74,000 MIPS on an RTX 4090 with near-ideal parallel scaling (the
HVM2 paper, docs.rs/crate/hvm/latest/source/paper/PAPER.pdf).

## §15. Code as physics simulation; cellular automata as substrate

Hashlife (Bill Gosper, 1984), Conway's Life Turing-completeness
(Rendell, 2002; Cook 2004 for Rule 110), Wireworld, the SAT-instance
"Tetris is Turing-complete" results — the lineage is well-known. The
serious question is **whether a cellular-automaton-like substrate is
a practical computational model** at scale.

Speculative but principled: certain GPU-resident workloads (lattice
Boltzmann, FDTD electromagnetics, agent-based simulations) are
essentially CA. The Halide compiler's stencil-scheduling work and the
Devito project (CGO 2019, devitoproject.org) show that the CA-style
abstraction maps efficiently onto modern hardware. A language that
treats **stencil computation as a base primitive** with explicit halo
regions and update rules is essentially "code as physics simulation
as compilation target."

The bridge to §41: the HVM2 interaction-combinator runtime is
*structurally* a parallel rewrite system on a graph — which is
exactly what a CA is, with a different rewrite rule. A sovereign IR
that supports both is a single graph-rewrite engine.

## §16. Topological and geometric programming

Geometric algebra (Clifford algebra) as a unifying numerical type is
the agenda of Hestenes, Doran/Lasenby, and Dorst/Fontijne/Mann (the
GA4CS textbook). Concrete proposals:

- **GAALOP** (Hildenbrand) — optimizing compiler for GA expressions
  to scalar/SIMD code.
- **Klein** (Jeremy Ong, 2020) — projective GA library in C++ with
  SIMD.
- **Bivector.net** / **Ganja.js** — pedagogical infrastructure.

The pitch is that 3D rotations, reflections, and transformations are
*messier* in matrix algebra than they need to be — rotors (the GA
equivalent of quaternions) compose more cleanly and the same operator
handles all dimensions. For a language whose application targets
include 3D simulation / rendering / robotics (Infinite Labyrinth,
etc.), making GA a first-class type system feature has real ergonomic
value.

**Topological data analysis** (Carlsson, Ghrist; persistent homology)
and **knot theory in code** (braided monoidal categories, anyonic
computation) are more speculative. The convergence point: braided
monoidal categories *are* the algebra of quantum gates and the
algebra of anyonic computation, so a language that takes ZX-calculus
seriously (per §9) has a path here.

## §17. AI-native source: LLMs as compiler components

The empirical situation as of mid-2026 is that LLMs are increasingly
competent as both:
- **Source generators** (Copilot, Cursor, Claude Code, Aider, Phind,
  Magicoder, Cody, etc.)
- **Compiler components** (neural superoptimization — papers like
  Bansal & Aiken's STOKE 2014, then ML-augmented variants; learning
  peephole optimizers; learning heuristics for register allocation;
  learned cost models for autotuning).

The non-obvious direction the user's question invites: **programs
that are partly text and partly neural weights.** This is plausibly
achievable via:

- **Differentiable programming** as the source representation (JAX,
  Zygote, Enzyme, Diffractor, Tapenade — see §20). The Zygote.jl
  paper (Innes et al., 2019) and the Enzyme paper (Moses & Churavy,
  NeurIPS 2020; Moses et al., SC2021 "Reverse-Mode Automatic
  Differentiation and Optimization of GPU Kernels via Enzyme")
  demonstrate that AD can apply to *arbitrary* code, including LLVM-
  IR-level kernels. Enzyme's distinguishing property is that it
  differentiates *after* optimization, which avoids the "AD before
  optimization" performance penalty by 4.2× in the original
  benchmarks.
- **Weights as values** in the language — a `Model[I, O]` is a typed
  function with a learnable parameter capability.
- **Inference as an effect** — calling an LLM is a `<llm_query>`
  effect that can be tracked, capability-gated, and budgeted.

The Prime Directive interaction is sharp here: LLM-as-compiler is
*the* path to consent-as-OS because the AI fleet is a partner in
language evolution. The capability-system layer (§36) ensures the
AI's edits go through explicit consent — the AI proposes a content-
addressed patch, the human (or governance system) approves it, and
the hash transitions.

## §18. Self-modifying canonical forms

The lineage runs Lisp → MetaOCaml → Template Haskell → Racket →
Lean 4 → Idris 2. The clean modern reference is **Lean 4
metaprogramming**: the elaborator is a monad transformer stack
(CoreM ⊂ MetaM ⊂ TermElabM ⊂ TacticM), and tactics, macros, syntax
extensions, and the elaborator itself are all just Lean programs.
Lean 4 is self-hosted: the Lean 4 compiler is written in Lean 4. The
metaprogramming book (leanprover-community.github.io/lean4-
metaprogramming-book) is the practical reference; the Lean 4 design
paper (de Moura & Ullrich, CADE 2021) is the formal one.

What this enables that LISP didn't: **proofs about the macros**.
F* tactics, Lean 4 tactics, and Agda reflection all let the
metaprogram be type-checked against the meta-theory of the host
language. A macro that constructs a proof can be checked for
soundness.

The asymptotic frontier: a language where **the rewrite rules of the
canonical hypergraph are themselves first-class values**, version-
controlled by hash, and the language can rewrite *its own AST* during
execution. Combined with content-addressing (§12), this means a
function's hash changes when it rewrites itself — preserving
auditability. Combined with capability security (§36), the right to
rewrite is itself a capability, so the AI fleet cannot silently
mutate code without consent.
